WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.820
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers Episode 126.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.300
It's July 9th, 2026.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.460
Here's your space and science briefing.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.220
A Falcon 9 booster has now completed its 36th flight and landing,

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.720
carrying 29 more Starlink satellites into orbit.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.400
The booster B-1067 lifted off from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.260
at 0925 UTC, carrying the Starlink Group 10-42 mission.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.500
It touched down on the drone ship a shortfall of gravitas

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.380
after delivering its payload.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:40.780
This marks the first time any orbital-class booster has reached 36 flights and landings.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.320
The launch continues the steady cadence of constellation replenishment flights.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.580
Next, teams will inspect the booster for any signs of wear before assigning it to a future mission.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:57.480
Now, shifting focus from sustained reuse on one vehicle to what happens when a star

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.440
itself reaches the end of its life.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:05.760
Astronomers are examining how a planet remained intact after its star evolved into a white dwarf.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.460
The system shows the planet orbiting at a distance that allowed it to avoid engulfment

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.320
during the star's red giant phase.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.420
Observations reveal unusual atmospheric or orbital properties that challenge standard models

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.000
of post-main-sequence planetary survival.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.620
Follow-up spectroscopy is planned to determine the planet's current composition and temperature.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.020
On a different note, a spacecraft is approaching the edge of our own star's influence.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:39.200
NASA's Pluto probe is approaching the region where the solar wind gives way to interstellar space.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.940
The spacecraft will become only the third human-made object to cross this boundary

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.780
after Voyager 1 and 2.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.640
Instruments are already recording changes in particle populations and magnetic fields.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.680
Data from this crossing will refine understanding of the heliopause structure.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.980
Mission controllers expect several months of measurements before the craft moves deeper into

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.060
interstellar space.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.020
From leaving the solar system, the story turns to a crew returning from its farthest crewed

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:10.880
journey so far.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.720
The four Artemis-2 astronauts reunited with their spacecraft three months after completing

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.920
the mission's record-setting lunar flyby.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.800
The capsule had traveled farther from Earth than any crewed vehicle in history.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.200
Engineers are now reviewing post-flight data and hardware performance.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.840
This reunion allows the crew to inspect systems first-hand before the next phase of

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.600
vehicle processing. The experience feeds directly into preparations for the first crewed lunar

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:45.440
landing. Next up, researchers have run scenarios testing how different numbers of astronauts

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:51.020
affect mission success on the moon. The study found that operational design choices,

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:56.660
such as task scheduling and habitat layout, matter as much as psychological factors.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:03.060
Results suggest an optimal crew size balances workload distribution with available resources.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:07.920
NASA can apply these findings when finalizing Artemis-based requirements.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.020
Additional simulations will test longer duration stays.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.800
From surface operations planning, attention moves to mapping the universe's largest structures.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:23.340
A five-year survey has produced the largest catalog yet of massive galaxy clusters,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:29.460
some dating back nearly 8 billion years. The data set maps the universe's largest structures

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:35.160
in unprecedented detail. Hidden within the measurements is evidence that star formation rates

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.720
across cosmic time differed from earlier predictions. Teams are now cross-checking the

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:47.480
catalog against optical and x-ray observations. Future papers will examine how these clusters grew

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:53.060
through mergers. Shifting to a nearby planet whose formation story remains unresolved,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:01.640
the 11 Jupiter mass planet Beta-Pictoris B orbits its 23 million-year-old star on a 23-year path.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:07.920
Direct imaging since 2008 has tracked its motion within the system's iconic dust disc.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:14.140
Current data cannot yet distinguish between formation by core accretion or disc instability.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:19.240
Additional observations with next-generation instruments aim to measure the planet's

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:24.680
atmospheric composition and any remaining heat from formation. From formation puzzles,

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.040
the view turns to a simple naked eye alignment visible tonight. The planet Venus

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:37.660
stands 1.1 degrees north of the bright star regulus in LEO after sunset. Observers can watch the

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:43.860
pair slowly descend together in the western sky. The conjunction offers a simple naked eye alignment

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:49.940
visible from most locations. No special equipment is required beyond clear western horizons.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.200
From easy sky-watching, the focus moves to hidden effects on astronaut health.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:01.640
University of Central Florida researchers exposed liver cells to combine radiation and microgravity

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:07.140
conditions. The resulting gene expression patterns match those seen in blood samples from

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:12.760
actual astronauts. The work identifies specific pathways that could be targeted to protect

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:18.780
crews on long-duration missions. Follow-up studies will test whether the same signatures appear

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.960
after shorter flights. Next, infrared sound and seismic networks turned and otherwise invisible

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:31.720
daytime fireball over Alaska into a fully reconstructed event. A bright meteor crossed Alaska

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:38.340
skies while the sun was still up rendering optical cameras ineffective. Scientists instead used

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.440
low-frequency sound waves and ground tremors recorded by earthquake stations to reconstruct

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:49.360
the object's trajectory and breakup. The method captured the final seconds of atmospheric entry

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.820
in detail. This approach could provide routine detection of daytime or cloudy sky events worldwide.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.700
Researchers are now refining algorithms to distinguish fireballs from other acoustic sources.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:08.020
If you could shrink Earth's atmosphere to the size of a football stadium, the thin shell where

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:14.300
most meteors burn up would be only a few rows of seats thick. A daylight fireball releases energy

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:20.520
equivalent to hundreds or thousands of tons of TNT, yet its optical flash can be lost against

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:26.400
the sun. Instead, the event generates powerful pressure waves that propagate as infrasound,

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.820
frequencies below 20 hertz, traveling thousands of kilometers with little attenuation.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.780
These waves reach ground stations hours later, while the same shock also couples into the

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:44.500
solid Earth, registering as tiny seismic signals on earthquake sensors. One surprising number

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:50.340
stands out. A single station can sometimes locate the source to within tens of kilometers using

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:56.780
arrival time differences across a sparse network. The remaining mystery is why some fireballs produce

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:02.440
unusually strong seismic coupling while others of similar size remain nearly silent on the ground.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:08.100
Leaving researchers without a reliable way to predict which events will be easiest to study.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.960
From the oldest satellite still circling Earth, attention turns to a new national agency.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:21.360
Launched in 1958, the small sphere Vanguard 1 continues circling Earth more than six decades later.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.960
Its simple design and high orbit have allowed it to outlast every other early spacecraft.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:31.640
Radio tracking still confirms its position and slow orbital decay.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:37.180
No other artificial satellite from the first decade of spaceflight remains operational.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:43.640
On a different note, Singapore has formed a new space agency. The agency signed a cooperation

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:49.940
agreement with Japan Space Agency to support industry growth. Officials aim to develop local

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:56.120
capabilities in satellite manufacturing and data services. The agreement includes technology

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:02.580
exchanges and joint project planning. Early focus areas include Earth observation and communications

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:08.440
applications. From agency building, the story moves to growing food on another world.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:14.320
Japanese researchers built a low power system that extracts nitrogen directly from the atmosphere

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:20.860
and converts it into plant usable form. Tests in simulated lunar regolith successfully

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:26.240
grew rice plants. An unexpected side effect improved nutrient uptake in the crops.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.080
The technology could support future lunar greenhouses while also offering applications for

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:38.580
terrestrial farming in nutrient poor soils. From lunar agriculture, the view shifts to the earliest

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:44.880
building blocks of planets. A Southwest Research Institute study proposes that tiny moonlets once

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:51.460
orbited early asteroids and concentrated millimeter-sized spherical particles. These grains later

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:57.220
became the building blocks of the most common meteorite types. Computer models show how gravitational

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:03.480
interactions could sort and preserve the material. Laboratory analysis of meteorites will test

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:09.360
the predicted grain size distributions. From the earliest building blocks, data now reveal that

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:15.560
the Milky Way's outer arms extend farther than mapped. Chandra X-ray observatory data reveal

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:22.300
spiral arm structures reaching beyond previous mapped limits. The extended arms contain hot gas

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:28.260
and young stars at greater distances from the galactic center. Adjusting models of Milky Way

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:34.240
structure will affect estimates of total stellar mass and rotation curve. Additional observations

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:40.640
are scheduled to trace the arms in other wavelengths. Before we go, keep an eye on the next round of

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:46.060
Artemis processing updates as the Orion capsule moves into its next phase of preparation.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:53.380
That's fascinating frontiers for today. If you enjoyed this, a rating or review on Apple podcasts or

